WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.053
Cold reduces battery chemical performance and can cause voltage sag under load.

00:00:06.053 --> 00:00:14.795
Usable endurance may be shorter than the displayed charge suggests, especially during climb, acceleration, wind correction, or return.

00:00:14.795 --> 00:00:18.158
Follow approved battery-temperature and preconditioning guidance.

00:00:18.158 --> 00:00:26.004
Keep batteries within appropriate transport and staging conditions, confirm status before launch, and avoid improvised heating.

00:00:26.004 --> 00:00:30.712
Plan shorter flights and larger reserves in the cold.

00:00:30.712 --> 00:00:35.420
Heat stresses batteries, motors, electronics, mobile devices, and payloads.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:43.266
Direct sun, hot vehicles, repeated flights, hovering, and processor load can raise temperatures beyond ambient conditions.

00:00:43.266 --> 00:00:47.525
Warnings, throttling, shutdown, or accelerated aging may follow.

00:00:47.525 --> 00:00:51.784
Storage and transport are part of weather management.

00:00:51.784 --> 00:00:57.389
Protect batteries from physical damage, extreme temperature, moisture, and conductive materials.

00:00:57.389 --> 00:01:01.648
Use approved charging, storage, inspection, and retirement practices.

00:01:01.648 --> 00:01:09.494
Monitor battery percentage, voltage or cell information when available, temperature alerts, actual consumption, and unexpected changes.

00:01:09.494 --> 00:01:16.892
Compare outbound energy use with the return plan, especially when wind or payload load increases.

00:01:16.892 --> 00:01:21.151
Crew performance also changes in heat and cold.

00:01:21.151 --> 00:01:30.791
Gloves, glare, dehydration, fatigue, numb hands, and screen behavior can slow decisions, so plan shelter, breaks, and shorter operating periods.

00:01:30.791 --> 00:01:33.705
Conservative reserve margins preserve options.

00:01:33.705 --> 00:01:38.412
If battery behavior differs from the plan, recover early.

00:01:38.412 --> 00:01:48.500
Do not use a successful warm-weather flight as evidence that the same route is safe in cold, heat, or strong wind.
